{"kind":"request","text":"Claude: make the projection cheap enough to use in an artifact-heavy workroom.\n\nWhy. In /Users/hughpyle/play/dap the required acts are unusable. That workroom\nholds 7,027 events and 5,089 artifacts, and one read-only fold of it takes 18 s\nwarm and 24 s single-threaded on this machine. Folder.Append is not the cost:\nunder GOMAXPROCS=1, of 20.1 s of CPU samples, foldState.project holds 11.0 s and\nthe verified read and append together hold about 1.2 s. Folder.Projection()\nrecomputes everything after every append, so a resident that has already\nverified the log still pays that whole pass for each new act — and a submit\nthat cannot answer inside ten seconds is refused with the act's fate unknown.\n\nWhere the time goes, from the same profile:\n\n- pathCovers is 35 percent of all samples. It allocates\n  strings.TrimSuffix(successor, \"/\")+\"/\" on every call, which is\n  runtime.concatstring2 at 33 percent and most of the mark work behind it.\n- postReceiptAccounting is 36 percent. For every artifact published by a merge\n  receipt it walks the whole log interval from the receipt to that artifact and\n  calls closestCoveringPath on each record in it, so several successors of one\n  receipt each re-walk the same interval. hasPostReceiptLiveArtifact walks from\n  the receipt to the end of the log per protected sibling.\n- receiptCheckpointSettles and causesSettledAtReceipt re-walk the receipt's\n  whole basis closure, with a fresh visited map, once per successor artifact.\n- unsettledCommitmentEvents runs a second complete staleness pass inside\n  project, which already computed one.\n\nNone of this is dap's fault, and no amount of housekeeping there fixes it. An\nearlier lane in that repository published a large and unnecessary artifact set,\nbut retirement is retroactive bookkeeping: the records stay in the log and the\nfold still reads them, so the cost cannot be retired away. The envelope campaign\nmissed this because its synthetic corpus is artifact-poor and receipt-free: its\nwarm status is 17 ms at 50,000 events, while dap is 18 s at 7,027.\n\nOutcome. The projection of an artifact-heavy workroom is fast enough that a\ndurable act is an interactive operation rather than a timeout, and every\nprojection it produces is identical to the one produced today.","body":{"branch":"request/projection-cost","conditions":"1. The workroom projection of an artifact-heavy log is an order of magnitude cheaper: a full fold and projection of a corpus the shape of dap (about 7,000 events, about 5,000 artifacts, merge receipts present) completes in about 2 s or less on the machine the evidence names, measured the same way, and the report states the before and after numbers. 2. Every projection is unchanged. A differential test folds a corpus exercising merge receipts, covering directory paths, left-live siblings and succession debt, and asserts the optimized projection equals the one the previous implementation produces, field for field, including live_predecessors, succession_unrecorded, merge_left_live, stale, describes_superseded_world and world_superseded_at. 3. pathCovers allocates nothing, and a test pins its boundary cases: exact match, directory prefix, trailing slash on either side, and a sibling whose name merely starts with the same characters. 4. No memo outlives the fact it caches: a test appends further records after a projection and shows the next projection reflects them. 5. Named unit tests cover each optimized path, and a mutation of each new guard is shown to turn a test red. 6. docs/reference/performance.md records the measurement and the shape of the corpus it came from. 7. go test ./... green at the exact head; independent review; ratified; landed on main and pushed.","target_head":"ccd161c6caa71f800bfdf17796d11bb3419f181f","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}